---

<SummerLayout title="Colonialism in West Africa — Things Fall Apart" section="tfa" spoke="history">

  <!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       SPOKE HEADER
       ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <div class="tfa-spoke-header">
    <div class="tfa-spoke-header-inner">
      <span class="tfa-spoke-header-eyebrow">Things Fall Apart · Spoke 03</span>
      <h1 class="tfa-spoke-header-title">Colonialism in West Africa</h1>
      <p class="tfa-spoke-header-deck">How an empire reached a society it could not read — partitioning a continent in a Berlin map room, inventing chiefs for a people who had none, and reducing a whole world to a paragraph in an administrator's book.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="tfa-header-chevron" aria-hidden="true">
    <AkweteChevron width={1200} height={9} />
  </div>

  <!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       ZONE 1 — Partitioning a Continent · exclusive reveal
       ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section
    class="sr-scrolly-zone sr-scrolly-zone--map tfa-zone--map tfa-partition-zone"
    data-field="map"
    data-tempo="driving"
    data-ground="cream"
    data-text="tfa"
    data-screen-label="§1 Partitioning a Continent"
  >

    <div class="tfa-z2-intro">
      <SectionOpener
        text="tfa"
        eyebrow="1884–1885"
        heading="Partitioning a Continent"
        background="cream"
      />

      <div class="sr-prose-block sr-prose-block--center">
        <p>By the 1880s, European powers were competing intensely for African territory — its raw materials, its land, its strategic position, and the commercial routes to which it offered access. To prevent open conflict between European rivals, German Chancellor Otto von Bismarck convened a conference in Berlin beginning November 15, 1884. Fourteen nations attended.</p>
      </div>

      <figure class="tfa-figure tfa-figure--full">
        <img
          src="/assets/tfa/berlin-conference-1884.webp"
          alt="A black-and-white engraving of the Berlin Conference, 1884: European diplomats seated around a long table in a formal hall. A large map of Africa hangs on the wall behind them, one delegate gesturing toward it."
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption class="tfa-figcaption">
          <span class="tfa-fignum">Fig 01</span>The Berlin Conference, 1884–1885, as depicted in the German illustrated magazine <em>Die Gartenlaube</em>. Fourteen European nations gathered to divide Africa among themselves — Bismarck stands at the centre, the map of the continent on the wall behind them. The peoples whose land was being partitioned were not present. <em>Die Gartenlaube</em>, 1884. Public domain.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <div class="sr-zone-field sr-zone-map-field tfa-partition-field" data-reveal="exclusive">
      <svg
        class="sr-partition"
        data-partition-map
        viewBox="-16 -44 452 556"
        xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
        preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid meet"
        role="img"
        aria-label="A schematic map of Africa. First a continent of many peoples with no fixed external borders; then straight lines ruled across it from a Berlin origin point; then, by 1914, the continent divided into the holdings of seven European powers, with only Liberia and Ethiopia left free and Igboland absorbed into British territory."
      ></svg>
    </div>

    <div class="sr-zone-beats">
      <div class="sr-zone-beat sr-zone-beat--left" data-beat data-activates="part-living">
        <div class="sr-beat-card sr-beat-card--lead">
          <p>No African representatives were present. No African leaders were consulted.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="sr-zone-beat sr-zone-beat--right" data-beat data-activates="part-partition">
        <div class="sr-beat-card">
          <p>The conference established the principle of effective occupation: a European power could claim African territory only if it could demonstrate actual administrative control, not merely coastal presence. This principle accelerated what became known as the Scramble for Africa. By 1914, the conference participants and their successors had divided virtually all of Africa among themselves. Only Liberia and Ethiopia remained independent.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="sr-zone-beat sr-zone-beat--left" data-beat data-activates="part-1914">
        <div class="sr-beat-card">
          <p>Britain's position in the Nigeria region was formalized at Berlin. Through a combination of commercial enterprise — the Royal Niger Company, chartered in 1886 — and military pressure, Britain had been extending its influence along the Niger River for decades. The Berlin Conference ratified this dominance and created the framework within which the Protectorate of Southern Nigeria would be formally established in 1900.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="sr-zone-beat sr-zone-beat--right" data-beat data-activates="part-1914">
        <div class="sr-beat-card">
          <p>The borders drawn at Berlin and in subsequent negotiations were entirely indifferent to the political, ethnic, and cultural organization of the peoples being divided. The Igbo, Hausa, Yoruba, and hundreds of other peoples with distinct languages, cultures, and governance systems were consolidated into a single colonial entity whose borders reflected European negotiation, not African reality.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="tfa-partition-caption">The partition of Africa, 1884–1914. The map is schematic — it shows the division of the continent among European powers, not exact borders. Holdings and proportions are approximate. Site-produced SVG.</div>
    </div>

  </section>

  <SectionTerminator text="tfa" />
  <ZoneSeam text="tfa" groundFrom="cream" groundTo="tinted" />

  <!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       ZONE 2 — Indirect Rule · exclusive reveal
       ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section
    class="sr-scrolly-zone sr-scrolly-zone--map tfa-zone--map tfa-warrant-zone"
    data-field="map"
    data-tempo="driving"
    data-ground="tinted"
    data-text="tfa"
    data-screen-label="§2 Indirect Rule"
  >

    <div class="tfa-z2-intro">
      <SectionOpener
        text="tfa"
        eyebrow="The Administrative Strategy"
        heading="Indirect Rule"
        background="tinted"
      />

      <figure class="tfa-figure tfa-figure--wide" style="margin-top:var(--s-8);">
        <img
          src="/assets/tfa/lugard-emirs-london-zoo.png"
          alt="A black-and-white photograph in a garden: a row of northern Nigerian emirs in white robes and turbans standing alongside several European men in dark Edwardian suits. A bearded official with a cane stands at the centre, flanked by the robed leaders."
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption class="tfa-figcaption">
          <span class="tfa-fignum">Fig 03</span>The Sultan of Sokoto and the Emirs of Gwandu and Kano at the London Zoological Gardens with Frederick Lugard. The photograph captures the co-option dynamic of indirect rule: Nigerian leaders positioned alongside the man who structured their administrative subjugation. In northern Nigeria, this dynamic worked — there were emirs to co-opt. In Igboland, there were not. Wikimedia Commons. Public domain.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <div class="sr-zone-field sr-zone-map-field tfa-warrant-field" data-reveal="exclusive">
      <svg
        class="sr-warrant"
        data-warrant-map
        viewBox="0 0 460 400"
        xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
        preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid meet"
        role="img"
        aria-label="A schematic map of Igboland as many autonomous village councils. British authority reaches in from one administrative point but finds no chief to anchor to; warrant chiefs are then imposed, offset from the communities they claim to rule."
      ></svg>
      <div class="sr-zone-caption">Indirect rule in Igboland, late nineteenth to early twentieth century. Each marker is an autonomous Igbo village community. The map is schematic — it shows political organization, not geography. Site-produced SVG based on the historical record.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="sr-zone-beats">
      <div class="sr-zone-beat sr-zone-beat--left" data-beat data-activates="map-precolonial">
        <div class="sr-beat-card">
          <p>The British colonial strategy in Nigeria was built around the doctrine of indirect rule, developed by Frederick Lugard, who served as High Commissioner of Northern Nigeria from 1900 and as Governor-General of the amalgamated Nigeria from 1914 to 1919. The principle was economical: rather than deploying large numbers of expensive British administrators across a vast territory, rule through existing local authorities — co-opt indigenous leaders, place British officials above them, and use the existing hierarchy to collect taxes, maintain order, and administer colonial law.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="sr-zone-beat sr-zone-beat--right" data-beat data-activates="map-attempt">
        <div class="sr-beat-card">
          <p>In northern Nigeria, indirect rule worked. The Hausa and Fulani of the north had large, hierarchically organized Islamic emirates with established rulers — emirs — who could be absorbed into the colonial administrative framework. In Igboland, it was a catastrophe. The Igbo had no emirs. They had no kings, no paramount chiefs, no permanent rulers with authority over more than a single village. When the British arrived looking for chiefs to co-opt, they found village councils, age grades, and the egwugwu — institutions that did not fit the administrative model they needed.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="sr-zone-beat sr-zone-beat--center" data-beat data-activates="map-warrant">
        <div class="sr-beat-card">
          <p>Their solution was the warrant chief system: the British selected individuals — sometimes arbitrarily, often rewarding those most willing to collaborate — and issued them written warrants granting colonial authority over their communities. These warrant chiefs were frequently men with no traditional standing. Without traditional legitimacy, they depended entirely on British backing to maintain their authority, frequently using that backing to enrich themselves and settle personal scores. The system was corrupt by design.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

  </section>

  <!-- §2 coda — Obierika's lament -->
  <section
    class="sr-scrolly-zone sr-scrolly-zone--ground"
    data-field="ground"
    data-tempo="still"
    data-ground="tinted"
    data-text="tfa"
    data-screen-label="§2 Obierika's lament"
  >
    <div class="sr-zone-ground-content" style="padding-block:var(--s-12);">
      <PullQuote
        text="tfa"
        quote='"The white man is very clever. He came quietly and peaceably with his religion. We were amused at his foolishness and allowed him to stay. Now he has won our brothers, and our clan can no longer act like one."'
        attribution="— Things Fall Apart, Chinua Achebe (Obierika to Okonkwo)"
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- §2b — The Women's War -->
  <section
    class="sr-scrolly-zone sr-scrolly-zone--ground"
    data-field="ground"
    data-tempo="still"
    data-ground="tinted"
    data-text="tfa"
    data-screen-label="§2b The Women's War"
  >
    <div class="sr-zone-ground-content" style="padding-top:var(--s-6);">
      <div class="tfa-still">
        <span class="tfa-gloss">1929 · two decades after the novel</span>
        <div class="tfa-ww-block">
          <svg
            class="tfa-ww-svg"
            data-encircle
            viewBox="0 0 300 200"
            role="img"
            aria-label="A ring of mobilised women encircling a single warrant chief — the traditional women's sanction of sitting on a man, the form the Women's War took in 1929."
          ></svg>
          <div class="tfa-ww-marker">
            <hr class="ww-rule" />
            <div class="ww-num-row">
              <span class="ww-num">tens of thousands</span>
              <span class="ww-num-lbl">women mobilised through their own associations</span>
            </div>
            <div class="ww-num-row">
              <span class="ww-num">32</span>
              <span class="ww-num-lbl">killed at the Native Courts they marched against</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <p class="tfa-ww-cap">The women's traditional sanction — gathering at a man's compound to shame him from office, called <em>"sitting on a man"</em> — scaled into mass action against the warrant chiefs.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="sr-prose-block sr-prose-block--center">
          <p>The most dramatic demonstration of the warrant chief system's failure came in the Women's War (<em>Ogu Umunwanyi</em>) of 1929 — two decades after the novel's setting. Tens of thousands of Igbo and Ibibio women mobilized through traditional women's associations that the colonial administration had entirely failed to account for or understand. They marched to Native Court buildings, demanded the resignation of the warrant chiefs, and were met with force: thirty-two women were killed. In the aftermath, the British conducted a formal inquiry, acknowledged the system's failures, and began studying Igbo governance seriously for the first time. The system was eventually dismantled and replaced with a structure more attentive to actual Igbo political organization.</p>
        </div>

        <p class="tfa-marker-note">This event postdates the novel's setting, but it illustrates what the warrant chief system produced — and the kind of organized resistance that Okonkwo, in the novel's final section, fails to inspire.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <SectionTerminator text="tfa" />
  <ZoneSeam text="tfa" groundFrom="tinted" groundTo="cream" />

  <!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       ZONE 3 — Disruption Through Respect · still · cream
       ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section
    class="sr-scrolly-zone sr-scrolly-zone--ground"
    data-field="ground"
    data-tempo="still"
    data-ground="cream"
    data-text="tfa"
    data-screen-label="§3 Disruption Through Respect"
  >
    <div class="sr-zone-ground-content">

      <SectionOpener
        text="tfa"
        eyebrow="The Church"
        heading="Disruption Through Respect"
        background="cream"
      />

      <div class="tfa-still">
        <div class="sr-prose-block sr-prose-block--center">
          <p>The missionaries who arrive in <em>Things Fall Apart</em> are not uniformly brutal or contemptuous. Mr. Brown, the first missionary to establish a lasting church in Umuofia, is patient, genuinely curious, and willing to engage seriously with Igbo theology. He learns the language. He sits with the elder Akunna for long philosophical conversations. He treats Igbo religion with intellectual respect.</p>
        </div>

        <p class="tfa-pivot">He is also the most structurally effective agent of colonial disruption in the novel.</p>

        <div class="sr-prose-block sr-prose-block--center">
          <p>The Church Missionary Society and the Roman Catholic Mission arrived ahead of formal colonial administration in many areas, establishing schools and congregations before district commissioners appeared. Mission schools became the primary pathway to Western literacy and education, which quickly became the primary pathway to employment in the colonial administration. The missionaries were not simply converting souls; they were producing the administrative class that would govern colonial Nigeria.</p>
        </div>

        <figure class="tfa-church-block">
          <svg
            class="sr-church-pipe"
            data-church-pipe
            viewBox="0 0 600 240"
            xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
            preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid meet"
            role="img"
            aria-label="A left-to-right pipeline of five stages — mission church, mission school, literacy, colonial post, the administrative class. The line and the figures begin warm and harden stage by stage into the cold colonial apparatus."
          ></svg>
          <figcaption class="tfa-church-cap">The mission school was the only road to literacy, and literacy the only road to colonial employment. The soft entry and the hard structure are <em>one line</em>: what begins as Mr. Brown's benevolent church hardens, stage by stage, into the administrative class that would govern colonial Nigeria.</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <span class="tfa-gloss">Who the church reached first</span>
        <div class="sr-prose-block sr-prose-block--center">
          <p>The church also specifically targeted the margins of Igbo society — the <em>osu</em> (outcasts), the mothers of twins, those who had suffered most under Igbo hierarchical norms. The church offered them dignity, inclusion, and a community outside the system that had excluded them. Nwoye, who cannot meet his father's demands for masculine achievement, finds in the church a different set of values and a different community. His conversion is not simply religious; it is an escape from a structure within which he cannot succeed.</p>
        </div>

        <figure class="tfa-church-block">
          <svg
            class="sr-church-path"
            data-church-path
            viewBox="0 0 460 348"
            xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
            preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid meet"
            role="img"
            aria-label="A schematic map of Umuofia as a cluster of communities. The church never reaches for a centre; a single continuous path threads the margins, reaching the people the clan had pushed out — among them Nwoye."
          ></svg>
          <figcaption class="tfa-church-cap">Each community holds firm at its <em>titled centre</em>. The church never reaches for the centre. Its single path runs the <em>margins</em>, reaching first the people the clan itself had pushed out — the <em>osu</em>, the mothers of twins, a son like Nwoye who could not meet his father's measure.</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <PullQuote
          text="tfa"
          quote='"It was not the mad logic of the Trinity that captivated him. It was the poetry of the new religion, something felt in the marrow."'
          attribution="— Things Fall Apart, Chinua Achebe"
        />

        <span class="tfa-gloss tfa-gloss--muted">Achebe's most uncomfortable argument</span>
        <div class="sr-prose-block sr-prose-block--center">
          <p>This is one of Achebe's most uncomfortable arguments: colonialism succeeded partly because the culture it displaced had its own exclusions, and some of the people most desperately seeking escape from those exclusions were willing to accept the alternative the missionaries offered. The church exploited not only Igbo culture's strengths but also its genuine failures.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>

  <SectionTerminator text="tfa" />
  <ZoneSeam text="tfa" groundFrom="cream" groundTo="tinted" />

  <!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       ZONE 4 — What Replaced What · still · tinted
       ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section
    class="sr-scrolly-zone sr-scrolly-zone--ground"
    data-field="ground"
    data-tempo="still"
    data-ground="tinted"
    data-text="tfa"
    data-screen-label="§4 What Replaced What"
  >
    <div class="sr-zone-ground-content">

      <SectionOpener
        text="tfa"
        eyebrow="The Colonial Replacement"
        heading="What Replaced What"
        background="tinted"
      />

      <div class="tfa-still">
        <div class="sr-prose-block sr-prose-block--center">
          <p>Colonial administration did not simply add new institutions alongside existing ones. It replaced Igbo institutions, function by function, with colonial equivalents that operated on entirely different principles.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tfa-rep-block">
          <div
            class="tfa-rep"
            data-replacement
            role="img"
            aria-label="A two-column diagram: each Igbo institution and the colonial form that replaced it. Egwugwu courts to the Native Court system; oracle war sanction to a colonial prohibition on conflict; village council authority to the warrant chief system; the communal social fabric to a convert and non-convert division."
          ></div>
          <p class="tfa-rep-cap">Colonial administration did not merely add new structures to Igboland. It replaced the institutions through which Igbo political, judicial, and social life functioned. What remained was not Igbo governance modified by colonial presence — it was <em>colonial governance occupying the space where Igbo governance had been.</em></p>
        </div>

        <div class="sr-prose-block sr-prose-block--center">
          <p>The District Commissioner who appears in the novel's final section considers himself a reasonable man, engaged in a civilizing project. He has spent years bringing order to different parts of Africa, and he plans to write a book about his experience. Its title — <em>The Pacification of the Primitive Tribes of the Lower Niger</em> — is Achebe's summary of the self-image of the entire colonial project. The title encodes every assumption the novel has spent two hundred pages dismantling: that the Igbo were primitive, that what happened to them was pacification, and that the story belongs to the man with the pen.</p>
        </div>

        <!-- The disappearance close -->
        <div class="tfa-close">
          <div class="tfa-close-variant tfa-close-variant--disappearance">
            <figure class="cc-disappear-quote">
              <div class="cc-dq-rule"></div>
              <blockquote>"The story of this man who had killed a messenger and hanged himself would make interesting reading. One could almost write a whole chapter on him. Perhaps not a whole chapter but <span class="cc-hinge">a reasonable paragraph</span>, at any rate. There was so much else to include, and one must be firm in <span class="cc-cut">cutting out details</span>."</blockquote>
              <figcaption>Things Fall Apart, Chinua Achebe · the District Commissioner, closing page</figcaption>
            </figure>
            <figure class="cc-disappear">
              <svg
                class="cc-disappear-svg"
                viewBox="0 0 840 380"
                data-diminish
                role="img"
                aria-label="Three figures of Okonkwo, left to right. At left, a large terra figure braced like a wrestler stands wrapped in flame and ringed by his clan. In the middle the same figure is smaller and cooled to brown. At right he is a single small umber mark embedded in a block of grey administrative prose — a reasonable paragraph."
              ></svg>
              <figcaption class="cc-disappear-cap">At the centre stands the Okonkwo of Part One — braced to grapple, the wrestler who threw Amalinze the Cat, his whole figure wrapped in the flame his people named him for: the <em>Roaring Flame</em>. In the middle the fire cools and the detail falls away. By the last he is what the District Commissioner leaves of him — a bare mark in a paragraph, <em>cold, impotent ash</em>.</figcaption>
            </figure>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <EndSignature text="tfa" />

    </div>
  </section>

  <SpokePagination
    section="tfa"
    prev={{ slug: 'culture', title: 'Igbo Society and Cosmology', number: 2 }}
    next={{ slug: 'figures', title: 'Key Figures', number: 4 }}
  />

</SummerLayout>

